Ezero - an easy language built on Zero

Documentation uses the original ZTEXT001 container with a length and checksum.
Read this document with: zero-text read ezero/README.ztext

Zero is the system language. Ezero adds automatically managed values, text
without required quotes, typed phrases, lists, functions and simple control
actions. Its frontend, package writer and runtime are original Zero code,
built by the self-hosted Zero compiler. No external language engine, compiler
backend or runtime library is used.

Native compiler commands
The native toolkit supplies zero/ezero on Linux and zero.exe/ezero.exe on
Windows. Place the command and its lib directory together, then compile:

    ezero examples/hello.ezero hello

Run the resulting hello executable normally on its target system. An existing
output is preserved; choose a new output filename for each compilation. The
commands perform the complete compilation pipeline in-process. See
../tools/compiler-cli.ztext for targets and ../tools/build.ztext for the original
Zero source-build command. Examples below use Ezero's own syntax.

A first program
    Mira => name
    Hello name => show

Mira is unquoted text. name becomes a binding and later reads its stored value.
Hello name is a phrase that prints Hello Mira.

Numbers remain numbers inside phrases
    3 bananas => purchase
    purchase => show
    purchase[1] + 2 => show
    purchase[2] => show

This prints 3 bananas, 5, and bananas. The first part remains the number 3.
Indices start at 1; zero and indices beyond size are errors. UTF-8 text is
supported without imposing a language on the system interface.

Two languages, the same result
Ezero:
    3 => repeat [ Hello => show ]

Zero:
    0 => @count.
    0 => @position.
    "Hello\n" => @message.
    ?? @count < 3 [
        0 -> @position.
        ?? :load8[@message + @position] != 0 [
            !put[:load8[@message + @position]].
            @position + 1 -> @position.
        ]
        @count + 1 -> @count.
    ]

Both print Hello three times. Ezero manages traversal, formatting and storage.
See LANGUAGE.ztext and examples/README.ztext for complete examples.

Files and volumes
read/readfile, save, writefile, create, del/rm, watch, search, go, disks and
fileexists use the original Zero native adapters or Zero OS kernel service.
watch/search/disks return ordinary one-based lists. save replaces content;
writefile exclusively creates a new file. These APIs use raw file contents;
they do not implicitly encode the ZTEXT001 document format. HOST.ztext states the exact native
and guest limits, error behavior and preservation guarantees. arguments() is
native-only; host() distinguishes Linux, Windows and Zero OS.

lines(text) splits LF/CRLF text; join(list | separator) joins text items.
They compose with file reads and writes, and run on every supported target.

Implementation and current scope
The frontend produces bounded instructions and data. pack.zero emits their
initialization as Zero source. The original Zero runtime and initialization
are compiled into a standalone Linux ELF, Windows PE or Zero OS application.
The executable interprets its embedded instruction package; Ezero does not yet
emit native instructions for every expression. It needs no separately installed
language runtime. The Zero OS compiler bundle contains native 32-bit and 64-bit
applications and supports bounded inline source. Guest applications share
kernel memory; this is not protected process isolation.

Immutable text, lists and phrases use two 262144-byte copying heap semispaces.
Live objects plus the next allocation must fit one space. MEMORY.ztext documents
frames, stacks, garbage collection and limits. Exhaustion is a reported error.
Ezero currently has no modules, closures, classes, exceptions, threads,
floating-point numbers, signed numbers, division or window/network APIs.

Own-language checks are documented in tests/README.ztext. Completed verification
reports state their actual scope; a passing finite suite does not prove the
absence of every defect. Existing Zero and Zero OS remain the foundation;
Ezero does not replace or rename their language or compiler.
